\chapter{Recogniser, stage two}

Stage two widens the recogniser from two knots to the taught set, and from one rope
to a rope tied to anchors. It adds the Alexander polynomial, which separates knots
that share a determinant. It adds anchors as closed loops, so a rope hitched on an
anchor is recognised as a link. And a test checks at build time that no two
library entries share both invariants.

The two open decisions were settled as recommended. A link is recognised by its
determinant. Anchors are stored as closed polylines for recognition, and the
physics shapes stay separate.

\section{Alexander polynomial}

The determinant alone cannot separate every taught knot. The Alexander polynomial
is the second invariant. It comes from the same crossing data as the Fox matrix,
through the Wirtinger relations. At a positive crossing with over arc $o$, incoming
under arc $i$ and outgoing under arc $j$, the Alexander row has $1-t$ at $o$, $t$ at
$i$ and $-1$ at $j$. A negative crossing gives $t-1$, $1$ and $-t$, which is the same
relation multiplied by a unit. The determinant of the matrix with one row and one
column removed is the polynomial, up to a unit $\pm t^k$.

The polynomial is found by evaluating that determinant at $k$ integer points and
interpolating. The determinant at each point is an exact integer, so the
interpolation is exact. The result is normalised: its lowest power is zero, and its
first coefficient is positive. Two knots match only when the determinant and the
normalised polynomial both agree. Mirror images agree on both, so they still match.

@d Recogniser: Alexander polynomial @{
import type { Diagram } from './crossings';
import { relationsOf } from './crossings';

// A polynomial in t, lowest power first.
type Poly = number[];

function addPoly(a: Poly, b: Poly): Poly {
  const n = Math.max(a.length, b.length);
  return Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => (a[i] ?? 0) + (b[i] ?? 0));
}

// The Alexander matrix: one row per crossing, one column per arc, with polynomial entries.
function alexanderMatrix(diagram: Diagram): Poly[][] {
  const k = diagram.crossings.length;
  const matrix: Poly[][] = Array.from({ length: k }, () => Array.from({ length: k }, (): Poly => []));
  relationsOf(diagram).forEach((rel, row) => {
    const entries: [number, Poly][] =
      rel.sign === 1
        ? [
            [rel.overArc, [1, -1]],
            [rel.inArc, [0, 1]],
            [rel.outArc, [-1]],
          ]
        : [
            [rel.overArc, [-1, 1]],
            [rel.inArc, [1]],
            [rel.outArc, [0, -1]],
          ];
    for (const [col, poly] of entries) matrix[row][col] = addPoly(matrix[row][col], poly);
  });
  return matrix;
}

function evalAt(p: Poly, x: bigint): bigint {
  let v = 0n;
  for (let i = p.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) v = v * x + BigInt(p[i]);
  return v;
}

// Exact determinant of an integer matrix, by the Bareiss method.
function bareissBig(input: bigint[][]): bigint {
  const m = input.map((row) => [...row]);
  const n = m.length;
  let sign = 1n;
  let previous = 1n;
  for (let k = 0; k < n - 1; k++) {
    if (m[k][k] === 0n) {
      const swap = m.findIndex((row, i) => i > k && row[k] !== 0n);
      if (swap === -1) return 0n;
      [m[k], m[swap]] = [m[swap], m[k]];
      sign = -sign;
    }
    for (let i = k + 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let j = k + 1; j < n; j++) {
        m[i][j] = (m[i][j] * m[k][k] - m[i][k] * m[k][j]) / previous;
      }
    }
    previous = m[k][k];
  }
  return n === 0 ? 1n : sign * m[n - 1][n - 1];
}

// Multiplies a polynomial by (t + c).
function timesLinear(poly: bigint[], c: bigint): bigint[] {
  const out = new Array<bigint>(poly.length + 1).fill(0n);
  poly.forEach((v, m) => {
    out[m + 1] += v;
    out[m] += v * c;
  });
  return out;
}

// The polynomial with integer coefficients through the points (xs[i], ys[i]).
function interpolate(xs: bigint[], ys: bigint[]): bigint[] {
  const denom = xs.map((xi, i) => xs.reduce((acc, xj, j) => (j === i ? acc : acc * (xi - xj)), 1n));
  const common = denom.reduce((acc, d) => acc * d, 1n);
  const total = new Array<bigint>(xs.length).fill(0n);
  xs.forEach((_, i) => {
    let numerator: bigint[] = [1n];
    xs.forEach((xj, j) => {
      if (j !== i) numerator = timesLinear(numerator, -xj);
    });
    const weight = (common / denom[i]) * ys[i];
    numerator.forEach((c, m) => {
      total[m] += c * weight;
    });
  });
  return total.map((c) => c / common);
}

// Lowest power zero, and the first coefficient positive.
function normalise(p: number[]): number[] {
  let lo = 0;
  while (lo < p.length && p[lo] === 0) lo++;
  let hi = p.length;
  while (hi > lo && p[hi - 1] === 0) hi--;
  const out = p.slice(lo, hi);
  if (out.length === 0) return [0];
  return out[0] < 0 ? out.map((c) => -c) : out;
}

// The Alexander polynomial of a knot diagram, normalised.
export function alexanderOf(diagram: Diagram): number[] {
  const k = diagram.crossings.length;
  if (k === 0) return [1];
  const matrix = alexanderMatrix(diagram);
  const minor = matrix.slice(0, k - 1).map((row) => row.slice(0, k - 1));
  // The minor's determinant has degree at most k - 1, so k sample points fix it.
  const xs = Array.from({ length: k }, (_, i) => BigInt(i + 2));
  const ys = xs.map((x) => bareissBig(minor.map((row) => row.map((entry) => evalAt(entry, x)))));
  return normalise(interpolate(xs, ys).map((c) => Number(c)));
}

export function samePolynomial(a: readonly number[], b: readonly number[]): boolean {
  return a.length === b.length && a.every((c, i) => c === b[i]);
}
@}

\section{Taught library}

The library is the set of knots the lessons teach, and of links a rope makes with
an anchor. Each knot entry has a name, a determinant and a normalised Alexander
polynomial. The values are those of the knots themselves, and the tests below
compute them from scripted ropes, so a wrong value shows as a failing test.

The stage-two knots are the unknot, the trefoil (the overhand) and the
figure-eight. The figure-eight is the knot of the research's table for the
figure-eight on a bight. The anchoring, belaying and rappel lessons add more in
stage six, and each addition must pass the collision check below.

The link entry is the hitch. A rope making one turn around an anchor forms a link
with determinant two.

@d Recogniser: taught library @{
export interface KnotEntry {
  name: string;
  determinant: number;
  alexander: readonly number[];
}

export const LIBRARY: readonly KnotEntry[] = [
  { name: 'unknot', determinant: 1, alexander: [1] },
  { name: 'trefoil', determinant: 3, alexander: [1, -1, 1] },
  { name: 'figure-eight', determinant: 5, alexander: [1, -3, 1] },
];

export interface LinkEntry {
  name: string;
  determinant: number;
}

// A rope hitched on an anchor. Determinant two is the Hopf link.
export const LINKS: readonly LinkEntry[] = [{ name: 'hitch', determinant: 2 }];
@}

\section{Anchors as closed loops}

Each anchor is a closed polyline for recognition. The last point joins the first.
A bar is a loop from its two ends back through its support. A ring lies in a plane,
a tree loop lies round a vertical trunk, and a carabiner is a loop with two round
ends. The physics shapes of the scene stay separate. Only the recogniser sees these
loops.

@d Recogniser: anchors as components @{
import type { Vec3 } from '../rope/snapshot';

const SAMPLES = 48;

// A bar: the segment from a to b, then back through a support offset by s.
export function barLoop(a: Vec3, b: Vec3, s: Vec3): Vec3[] {
  const add = (p: Vec3, q: Vec3): Vec3 => ({ x: p.x + q.x, y: p.y + q.y, z: p.z + q.z });
  return [a, b, add(b, s), add(a, s)];
}

// A ring in the xy plane, round a centre.
export function ringXY(centre: Vec3, radius: number): Vec3[] {
  return Array.from({ length: SAMPLES }, (_, i) => {
    const t = (2 * Math.PI * i) / SAMPLES;
    return { x: centre.x + radius * Math.cos(t), y: centre.y + radius * Math.sin(t), z: centre.z };
  });
}

// A loop round a vertical trunk: a ring in the xz plane.
export function treeLoop(centre: Vec3, radius: number): Vec3[] {
  return Array.from({ length: SAMPLES }, (_, i) => {
    const t = (2 * Math.PI * i) / SAMPLES;
    return { x: centre.x + radius * Math.cos(t), y: centre.y, z: centre.z + radius * Math.sin(t) };
  });
}

// A carabiner's loop, seen face-on: two straight sides with round ends at x = plus or minus halfLength.
export function carabiner(centre: Vec3, halfLength: number, radius: number): Vec3[] {
  const half = SAMPLES / 2;
  const right = Array.from({ length: half }, (_, i) => {
    const t = -Math.PI / 2 + (Math.PI * i) / (half - 1);
    return { x: centre.x + halfLength + radius * Math.cos(t), y: centre.y + radius * Math.sin(t), z: centre.z };
  });
  const left = Array.from({ length: half }, (_, i) => {
    const t = Math.PI / 2 + (Math.PI * i) / (half - 1);
    return { x: centre.x - halfLength + radius * Math.cos(t), y: centre.y + radius * Math.sin(t), z: centre.z };
  });
  return [...right, ...left];
}
@}

\section{Tests}

The stage-two tests are the proof for the stage. Each library knot is recognised
from a scripted rope of that knot. The Alexander polynomials are computed and
compared with the library. A rope turned round a bar is a link with the bar, and a
rope clear of the bar is not. The collision check fails if two library entries
share both invariants.

The figure-eight curve is the standard parametrisation, sampled at 120 points.
The hitch is one turn of rope round the bar.

@d Recogniser: stage two tests @{
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import type { Vec3 } from '../rope/snapshot';
import { barLoop, ringXY } from '../anchors/shapes';
import { alexanderOf, samePolynomial } from './alexander';
import { diagramOf } from './crossings';
import { LIBRARY, LINKS } from './library';
import { recognise } from './recogniser';

const SAMPLES = 120;

function sample(curve: (t: number) => Vec3): Vec3[] {
  return Array.from({ length: SAMPLES }, (_, i) => curve((2 * Math.PI * i) / SAMPLES));
}

const trefoil = (t: number): Vec3 => ({
  x: Math.sin(t) + 2 * Math.sin(2 * t),
  y: Math.cos(t) - 2 * Math.cos(2 * t),
  z: -Math.sin(3 * t),
});

const figureEight = (t: number): Vec3 => {
  const r = 2 + Math.cos(2 * t);
  return { x: r * Math.cos(3 * t), y: r * Math.sin(3 * t), z: Math.sin(4 * t) };
};

// The bar runs along z, and its support hangs off to the side at y = -0.6.
const bar = barLoop({ x: 0, y: 0, z: -0.4 }, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0.4 }, { x: 0, y: -0.6, z: 0 });

describe('stage two recogniser', () => {
  it('names the figure-eight from its scripted rope', () => {
    const result = recognise({ points: sample(figureEight), radius: 0.02 });
    expect(result.name).toBe('figure-eight');
    expect(result.determinant).toBe(5);
  });

  it('gives the trefoil and the figure-eight their Alexander polynomials', () => {
    expect(alexanderOf(diagramOf([sample(trefoil)]))).toEqual([1, -1, 1]);
    expect(alexanderOf(diagramOf([sample(figureEight)]))).toEqual([1, -3, 1]);
  });

  it('names a rope turned once round the bar as a hitch', () => {
    const turn = Array.from({ length: 40 }, (_, i) => {
      const t = (2 * Math.PI * i) / 40;
      return { x: 0.3 * Math.cos(t), y: 0.3 * Math.sin(t), z: 0 };
    });
    const result = recognise({ points: turn, radius: 0.02 }, [bar]);
    expect(result.anchors[0]).toEqual({ name: 'hitch', determinant: 2 });
  });

  it('finds no link with a rope that is clear of the bar', () => {
    const clear = ringXY({ x: 2, y: 0, z: 0 }, 0.3);
    const result = recognise({ points: clear, radius: 0.02 }, [bar]);
    expect(result.anchors[0]).toEqual({ name: null, determinant: 0 });
  });

  it('keeps every library entry apart from the others', () => {
    for (let i = 0; i < LIBRARY.length; i++) {
      for (let j = i + 1; j < LIBRARY.length; j++) {
        const a = LIBRARY[i];
        const b = LIBRARY[j];
        const shared = a.determinant === b.determinant && samePolynomial(a.alexander, b.alexander);
        expect(shared, `${a.name} and ${b.name} share both invariants`).toBe(false);
      }
    }
  });

  it('keeps every link entry named once', () => {
    const names = LINKS.map((e) => e.name);
    expect(new Set(names).size).toBe(names.length);
  });
});
@}
